{"name":"Bali Hai","hex":"#849ca9","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hai","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Haida."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Hi."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hai"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bali Ha'i","blocks":[{"text":"\"Bali Ha'i\", also spelled \"Bali Hai\", is a show tune from the 1949 Rodgers and Hammerstein musical South Pacific. The name refers to a mystical island, visible on the horizon but not reachable, and was originally inspired by the sight of Ambae island from neighboring Espiritu Santo in Vanuatu, where author James Michener was stationed in World War II."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bali_Ha'i","wikidata":"Q4850834"},{"title":"Bali","blocks":[{"text":"Bali is an Indonesian island and province and the westernmost of the Lesser Sunda Islands. East of Java and west of Lombok, the province includes the island of Bali and a few smaller offshore islands, notably Nusa Penida, Nusa Lembongan, and Nusa Ceningan to the southeast. The provincial capital, Denpasar, is the most populous city in the Lesser Sunda Islands and the second-largest, after Makassar, in Eastern Indonesia. The Denpasar metropolitan area is the extended metropolitan area around Denpasar. The upland town of Ubud in Greater Denpasar is considered as Bali's cultural centre. The province is Indonesia's main tourist destination, with a significant rise in tourism since the 1980s, and has become the country's area of overtourism. Tourism-related business makes up 80% of the Bali economy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Bali_in_Indonesia_%28special_marker%29.svg/330px-Bali_in_Indonesia_%28special_marker%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bali","wikidata":"Q3125978"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/